---
import PageHeader from '~/components/PageHeader.astro';
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import {
  DIFFICULTY_BONUS,
  OSS_CAP,
  OSS_STATUS_FACTOR,
  PROVING_DAYS,
  PROVISIONAL_BELOW,
  TIERS,
  TRACK_FULL_DAYS,
  UNRATED_BELOW,
  WEIGHTS,
} from '~/lib/score';
import { SITE } from '~/lib/site';
import { CODEBASE_CRITERIA, CODEBASE_MAX, CODEBASE_META, TECHNIQUES, TECHNIQUE_META } from '~/lib/taxonomy';

const toc = [
  { id: 'principles', label: 'Principles' },
  { id: 'status', label: 'How status is derived' },
  { id: 'score', label: 'Security Score' },
  { id: 'open-source', label: 'Open source' },
  { id: 'proving', label: 'Proving & earned credit' },
  { id: 'transparency', label: 'Transparency & difficulty' },
  { id: 'challenges', label: 'Public challenges' },
  { id: 'evidence', label: 'Evidence rules' },
  { id: 'lab', label: 'The Lab' },
  { id: 'outputs', label: 'What we host' },
  { id: 'limits', label: 'Known limitations' },
];
---

<Base title="Methodology" description="How luau.site derives break status, computes the Security Score, and decides what counts as evidence.">
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Project"
    title="Methodology"
    description="Every number on luau.site comes from code you can read and data you can audit. Here's exactly how it works."
  />
  <div class="container-page grid gap-12 py-12 lg:grid-cols-[13rem_minmax(0,1fr)]">
    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="hidden lg:block">
      <ul class="sticky top-24 space-y-2 text-sm">
        {toc.map((t) => <li><a href={`#${t.id}`} class="text-muted hover:text-fg">{t.label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose-site max-w-[44rem]">
      <h2 id="principles">Principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Computed, not typed.</strong> Status, time to break and score are pure functions of the dataset (<a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/src/lib/status.ts`}><code>status.ts</code></a>, <a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/src/lib/score.ts`}><code>score.ts</code></a>), covered by unit tests.</li>
        <li><strong>Sourced or it doesn't ship.</strong> Every entry and every claim links to evidence. CI rejects data without it.</li>
        <li><strong>Public history.</strong> The data lives as YAML in a public repository, so every change is a reviewable commit.</li>
        <li><strong>No paid placement.</strong> Vendors can't buy a position or a removal. They can submit corrections and statements like anyone else.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="status">How status is derived</h2>
      <p>Each obfuscator <em>version</em> has one of three statuses, derived from the deobfuscators that target it:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Broken</strong></td><td>At least one tool has <em>full</em> support for this version and outputs readable (or near-original) source.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Partial</strong></td><td>Tools exist, but they only recover constants, bytecode or traces, or their support is partial or experimental.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Holding</strong></td><td>No public tool is tracked for this version. This means "none known", not "unbreakable".</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Open source</strong></td><td>Shown instead of Holding for open-source projects. When the source is public, it can never truly hold.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        The <strong>break date</strong> is the earliest date one of the breaking tools became public, either its first-seen date or a per-version
        <code>since</code> date. <strong>Time to break</strong> is the break date minus the release date. When either date is only month-precise or
        marked approximate (<code>~</code>), the duration is shown with a <code>~</code> and rounded to coarser units.
      </p>
      <p>
        A maintainer can override a derived status in rare cases, for example a break confirmed privately. An override must include a written note and
        sources, and it is labelled on the page.
      </p>

      <h2 id="score">Security Score</h2>
      <p>The score runs from 0 to 100 and has four components, all evaluated against the obfuscator's <em>current</em> version:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Weight</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Resistance</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.resistance}</td>
            <td>Holding 50 · broken only by private or paid tools 30 · partial 20 · broken by a free closed tool 5 · broken by an open-source tool 0. When several tools break it, the most widely available one counts. A broken version keeps a small bonus if even its easiest break was hard (see <a href="#transparency">difficulty</a>).</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Track record</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.track}</td>
            <td>Median <em>survival</em> across dated versions: time to break, or time since release if still unbroken. Full points at {TRACK_FULL_DAYS} days or more.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Lab results</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.lab}</td>
            <td>The share of our benchmark samples (for the current version) that <em>no</em> deobfuscator recovered with matching behavior.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Technique depth</strong></td><td>{WEIGHTS.technique}</td>
            <td>A capped checklist of documented techniques. VM virtualization counts most. Compression and watermarking count zero.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h3>Missing data, provisional and unrated scores</h3>
      <p>
        If a component has no data (no dated versions, no Lab runs, no documented techniques), it gets <strong>neutral half credit</strong>. We
        don't drop it and re-scale the rest, because missing evidence shouldn't push a score up or down. The share of the weight that is backed
        by real data is shown as <em>data %</em>.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Below {Math.round(PROVISIONAL_BELOW * 100)}%, the score is <strong>provisional</strong> and its tier is drawn with a dashed border.</li>
        <li>
          Below {Math.round(UNRATED_BELOW * 100)}%, the project is <strong>unrated</strong>: no score or tier is shown, and it's listed after every rated
          project. This keeps a brand-new entry we know almost nothing about from topping the rankings.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Tiers</h3>
      <p>{TIERS.map((t) => `${t.tier} ≥ ${t.min}`).join(' · ')}. Tiers are not shown for unrated projects.</p>
      <h3>Technique points</h3>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{TECHNIQUES.map((t) => <tr><td><a href={`/learn#${t}`}>{TECHNIQUE_META[t].label}</a></td><td>{TECHNIQUE_META[t].points}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="open-source">Open source</h2>
      <p>
        An open-source obfuscator can never truly hold. Anyone can read exactly how it transforms code, so "no public deobfuscator yet" means
        little. Open-source projects are scored differently:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Instead of Holding, they show the status <strong>Open source</strong>. Partial and Broken still apply when tools exist.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Resistance and track record are replaced by a Codebase grade</strong> worth {WEIGHTS.codebase} points. A maintainer grades
          five criteria from 0 to 4, for a total out of {CODEBASE_MAX}.
        </li>
        <li>
          The grade is multiplied by {OSS_STATUS_FACTOR.partial} if the latest version is partially broken, or {OSS_STATUS_FACTOR.broken} if it is
          broken.
        </li>
        <li><strong>No benefit of the doubt:</strong> missing Lab results or techniques earn nothing, instead of the usual neutral half credit.</li>
        <li>The total is <strong>capped at {OSS_CAP}</strong>, so open source can reach a decent score but never A or S.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The effect: an average open-source project scores about the same as a broken closed-source one, and only a genuinely strong codebase
        scores clearly higher.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>What a 4 looks like</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{CODEBASE_CRITERIA.map((c) => <tr><td><strong>{CODEBASE_META[c].label}</strong></td><td>{CODEBASE_META[c].description}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>

      <h2 id="proving">Proving period and earned credit</h2>
      <p>
        A new release with no tracked break hasn't proven anything yet. For its first {PROVING_DAYS} days, its Resistance ramps linearly from the
        previous version's Resistance up to the full 50. A first-ever release starts from 25. So when a broken obfuscator ships a fix, its score
        rises a little on day one and keeps rising every day the new version holds. A break ends the proving period immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Earned credit.</strong> Some obfuscators publish no release dates. For those, the clock starts when luau.site begins watching:
        either a recorded check that found no public deobfuscator, or the date an <a href="#challenges">open public challenge</a> was posted,
        whichever came first. Survival is rewarded once it has been observed, not assumed. Projects earning credit are always rated
        (provisionally if data is thin), because the ramp already keeps them from outranking proven projects on day one.
      </p>
      <p>Versions still in their proving period are left out of the track-record median until they have a settled outcome.</p>
      <p>Scores that depend on the date are rebuilt daily.</p>

      <h2 id="transparency">Transparency and break difficulty</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Transparency.</strong> When a vendor publishes no versions or changelog, a missing history earns no neutral credit; the track
          record is 0. Otherwise an opaque vendor would outscore one whose public history shows a fast break.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Difficulty.</strong> Breaks are tagged easy, moderate or hard, based on tool complexity, crackme ratings and how long a break took to
          appear. A broken version keeps +{DIFFICULTY_BONUS.moderate} for a moderate break and +{DIFFICULTY_BONUS.hard} for a hard one. The
          easiest break decides, and an untagged break counts as easy: once something is broken, there's no benefit of the doubt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Availability.</strong> Request-only and beta products are labelled as such. Limited availability means fewer people can attack
          real outputs, so it earns no points by itself. A public challenge is what lets such a product prove itself.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="challenges">Public challenges</h2>
      <p>
        Vendors sometimes publish crackmes or bounties protected by their own obfuscator. luau.site records them with their platform rating,
        downloads, writeups and bounty. An <strong>open</strong> challenge starts the earned-credit clock. A <strong>solved</strong> challenge is
        shown on the timeline, but it only counts as a break once a matching deobfuscator or public writeup is recorded, because one solved
        sample doesn't mean every script can be recovered.
      </p>

      <h2 id="evidence">Evidence rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Every obfuscator, deobfuscator, auth service and hand-written event needs at least one source with an access date. Archive links are encouraged.</li>
        <li>Primary sources (repositories, official docs, vendor changelogs) beat screenshots and second-hand reports.</li>
        <li>Entries carry a <code>verified</code> flag and a <code>lastVerified</code> date. After {SITE.staleAfterDays} days without re-verification, an entry is flagged as possibly outdated.</li>
        <li>A deobfuscator only counts once it's publicly verifiable: a repository, a public release, or a reproducible demonstration. Rumors don't count.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="lab">The Lab</h2>
      <p>
        The Lab is our own benchmark. We write small test scripts, obfuscate each one with every obfuscator, run every deobfuscator on the result, and
        then execute all three stages with the official <code>luau</code> CLI. A deobfuscation counts as <em>recovered</em> only if its output prints
        exactly what the original printed. See <a href="/lab">the Lab</a> for the corpus and how to contribute runs.
      </p>

      <h2 id="outputs">What we host, and what we don't</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We host our own corpus scripts and the outputs produced from them.</li>
        <li>We <strong>don't</strong> host third-party scripts, their deobfuscated output, or deobfuscator binaries. We link to the public source.</li>
        <li>Nothing here is meant to help anyone bypass licensing on a specific script. The goal is transparency about how strong protection actually is.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="limits">Known limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>"Holding" reflects what we know. Private tools that stay private are invisible to us.</li>
        <li>Release dates for many versions aren't publicly documented, so track record often rests on approximate dates.</li>
        <li>Lab coverage depends on maintainers running paid obfuscators by hand, so it grows over time.</li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
